===============================================================================================
B. NON-NEGOTIABLE RELEASE GATES
===============================================================================================
 B01. Never display a KPI marked VERIFIED if any critical source->engine->export value differs.
 B02. Preserve exact source timestamps including hours, minutes, seconds, milliseconds when
      available; preserve original text/precision alongside typed normalized representation.
 B03. 0 uncaught Excel errors in derived KPI/formula ranges: #VALUE!, #REF!, #DIV/0!,
      #NAME?, #NUM!, #N/A, #NULL!, and corrupted formula/cache states. Legitimate missing
      business values are blank/N/A with explicit semantics, never Excel error leakage.
 B04. Every derived row has a valid formula OR explicitly documented static materialization;
      no accidental formula-only-first-row behavior.
 B05. Exported dashboard, detail sheets, audit, chart caches and reference engine reconcile
      at full precision, allowing documented floating point tolerance.
 B06. Reopened denominator and SLA denominator must be explicit in labels/tooltips.
 B07. No synthetic money, sales, product or customer financial KPIs for Helpdesk.
 B08. Unknown schemas get generic truthful dashboard or user confirmation, never forced sales.
 B09. Do not claim "FULL RECALC SAFE" until Excel desktop has recalculated the workbook and
      saved/reopened with correct results. OOXML checks alone are insufficient.
 B10. All computations remain in browser; exports generated locally; no AI, no cloud data API.

===============================================================================================
C. TIMESTAMP LOSSLESS PIPELINE — DETAILED ENGINEERING REQUIREMENTS
===============================================================================================
 C01. Inventory data flow: CSV raw bytes -> delimiter/encoding parser -> typed values ->
      semantic mapping -> duration engine -> ReportSpec -> XLSX serialization -> formulas/
      cached results -> Excel recalculation -> charts -> audit. Log LOCAL redacted diagnostic
      trace at every boundary (field ID, row ID, type, timezone, precision, serialized value).
 C02. CSV parser must not convert datetime to date-only; recognize ISO 8601 date and datetime,
      timezone offsets, fractional seconds and quoted values. Preserve raw string verbatim.
 C03. Date-only and datetime are distinct types. Never call startOfDay(), floor(serial),
      toDateString(), date-only formatter, integer conversion, or Math.trunc() on timestamps
      used in durations. Date-only is allowed for calendar grouping AFTER metric computation.
 C04. Keep typed time as epoch milliseconds/microseconds where unambiguous, or an explicit
      timezone-naive local datetime structure when source timezone unspecified. Do not
      silently convert naive local timestamps to UTC or browser timezone.
 C05. Detect DST gaps/overlaps and timezone ambiguity; request user confirmation where
      elapsed time would change. For the fixture, use consistent naive elapsed calendar-time
      arithmetic if no timezone exists, disclose that policy.
 C06. XLSX uses numeric date-time serial with FRACTIONAL DAY: serial = date component +
      (hour*3600 + minute*60 + second + fractionalSecond)/86400. Retain precision at least
      to seconds (milliseconds where source has them). Format `yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss` or a
      locale-appropriate equivalent; display formatting alone must NOT mask lost serials.
 C07. Respect 1900 vs 1904 workbook epoch; handle Excel's 1900 leap-year compatibility
      without off-by-one mistakes. Ensure round-trip conversion is tested for both epochs.
 C08. Formula duration for valid completed row: (FinishSerial - StartSerial)*24 hours.
      Native JS duration: (finishMs-startMs)/3_600_000. The two must agree within tolerance.
 C09. Status and date validity: completed = terminal status AND valid completion timestamp.
      If terminal status conflicts with missing/invalid finish date, flag and exclude from
      duration/SLA denominator pending user confirmation; never silently treat as zero.
 C10. SLA met iff valid completed duration <= SLA Limit (hrs), with explicit equality case.
      SLA breach iff > limit. Exclude invalid durations and invalid limits with counts.
 C11. Guard negative, nonfinite, overflow and mixed-unit durations. Detect duration expressed
      as Excel day fraction vs hour count; never double-multiply or double-divide by 24.
 C12. Preserve timezone and as-of assumptions in report metadata and every export.
 C13. Test round trips with midnight, same-day, cross-day, month-end, leap day, 1900/1904,
      seconds, fractions, blank end, invalid input, negative duration, 0-hour completion,
      DST ambiguity, locale ambiguity and thousands of heterogeneous rows.
 C14. Golden case HD-000001: compare source values with normalized values, XLSX serials,
      rendered text, formula, cached value, engine duration and Excel recalculated duration.
      Expected 4.00 hours if source timestamps match observed example. Log a failed assertion
      on any mismatch, with no data leaked to remote telemetry.

===============================================================================================
D. EXCEL FORMULA FILL, TYPES, CACHES AND INTEROPERABILITY
===============================================================================================
 D01. Choose ONE tested strategy per derived column:
      (a) structured Excel Table calculated column, correctly filled through all data rows;
      (b) explicit per-row formulas with correctly adjusted relative references;
      (c) static precomputed typed values with formula definition/provenance and NO false
          promise of live Excel recalculation. Mixed strategies require deliberate metadata.
 D02. Never write formula only in row 2 and assume Excel/library autofill will occur.
      Verify each eligible row's formula or materialized value after workbook export.
 D03. Use real Excel numeric date-time cells (fractional serials) for computations; no
      text-to-date implicit coercion or locale-dependent DATEVALUE parsing in formulas.
 D04. Example formula logic (adjust to actual safe table references):
      =IF(OR([@[Logged On]]="",[@[Finished At]]=""),"",([@[Finished At]]-[@[Logged On]])*24)
      =IF([@ResolutionHours]="","",IF([@ResolutionHours]<=[@[SLA Limit (hrs)]],1,0))
      BUT include terminal-status validation and invalid-date guards; these examples are
      not complete business definitions or a substitute for typed canonical engine.
 D05. Formulas must use correct separators/function names for XLSX interoperability,
      preserve relative/absolute references, and avoid implicit intersection mistakes.
 D06. Scan worksheet XML for `<f>`, `<v>`, cell `t`, styles and shared/array formulas;
      count formulas by derived column and expected eligible rows; verify shared formula
      anchors and range boundaries. Inspect actual values, not only XML string presence.
 D07. Detect Excel errors in BOTH cached values and formula evaluation. Zero error target
      for supported data. Do not suppress #VALUE! by replacing it with invented 0.
 D08. Ensure chart series point to valid dynamic table/ranges and cached chart values agree
      with canonical aggregates; chart totals reconcile to their source population.
 D09. Recalculate and verify in actual Microsoft Excel desktop when available; test
      reopening saved file and changing a source value. Check automatic recalc settings,
      cached values and formulas after save. LibreOffice may be supplemental but not proof
      of Microsoft Excel parity.
 D10. When Excel execution unavailable, report "Excel desktop recalc NOT_TESTED"; OOXML
      inspection is not sufficient to label "recalc safe".
 D11. Prevent formulas in original untrusted CSV text from executing (CSV injection).
      Preserve source values and escape safely when writing cells.
 D12. Ensure row count 8,000 plus headers; no silent truncation, no extra fabricated rows,
      no source mutation. Preserve original 12 fields separately from derived fields.
 D13. Scan all sheets, all used ranges, all charts and defined names for #VALUE!, #REF!,
      broken external links, stale ranges, inappropriate domain labels and inconsistent KPIs.
 D14. Numeric precision: preserve double or decimal precision in canonical metrics;
      present 47.19% only after computing exact 2925/6199. Avoid integer truncation.
 D15. Export should be atomic: validate in memory before enabling verified download.
      If blocked, offer clearly labeled DRAFT with explanation, never false PASS.

