==============================================================================================
MASTER PRD v7.0 — JULIUS-PLUS, ZERO-AI, BROWSER-ONLY UNIVERSAL DASHBOARD PLATFORM
==============================================================================================
Product: excel.dattasable.com
Date: 23 October 2026
Audience: Codex, Antigravity, technical lead, QA, product owner
Priority: P0 critical semantic correctness, P1 three exporters and premium dashboard, P2 advanced UX.
Status: Implementation specification; NO features are claimed as already delivered.

MISSION
Transform arbitrary SUPPORTED structured tabular data into a reliable, aesthetically premium,
interactive business intelligence report in the user's browser; export consistent Excel (.xlsx),
PDF (.pdf) and portable HTML (.html). No generative AI, hosted inference, LLMs, API tokens,
cloud dataset processing or telemetry of data contents. The user should understand what each
metric means, where it came from, and what assumptions were made.

MANDATORY EXISTING-PRODUCT CONSTRAINTS
- EXTEND the existing codebase; preserve routes, auth if present, styles, working features,
  and 32 existing templates as OPTIONAL presentation presets, never forced domain logic.
- Do not replace existing framework, backend, hosting, database or working export flows unless
  demonstrably necessary and approved by owner.
- Detect existing technology from repository; don't assume library APIs or silently migrate.
- Existing production URLs and contracts must not break; use versioned adapters.
- Default to local-first; do not send file contents, derived values, names, metadata or report
  previews to server endpoints, analytics, logs, crash reporters, AI services or CDNs.
- The site's initial HTML/CSS/JS may be delivered from hosting; "local processing" means that
  the dataset remains inside the browser. "Offline capable" is a separate acceptance criterion.
- User permission is required for optional local persistence, download and any external share.

COMPETITIVE TARGET
Match the usefulness and information architecture of the provided one-page Julius Helpdesk
Operations Dashboard, then exceed it with semantic confidence, multi-domain universality,
editable native Excel, print-quality PDF, portable interactive HTML, complete audit trail,
editable visual layout, accessibility, privacy assurance, deterministic insight/action rules,
and one canonical metric definition for all three formats.

NON-GOALS
- Claiming to infer arbitrary unknown business meanings without ambiguity.
- Creating financial metrics when fields are absent; predicting targets or making up benchmarks.
- Claiming to be faster, more accurate, or more private than named competitors without tests.
- Cloning Julius proprietary interface/assets, copying branding, or reproducing its code.
- Supporting non-tabular PDF/image/audio as source datasets in the initial release.
- Making a web dashboard screenshot the only Excel deliverable.


==============================================================================================
01. JULIUS REFERENCE: WHAT THE USER-SUPPLIED PDF ACTUALLY SHOWS
==============================================================================================
Source: Helpdesk_Operations_Dashboard.pdf, page 1 (user-provided). These are benchmark
observations, NOT evidence of how Julius implemented its internals.
- Title: "Helpdesk performance dashboard"; 8,000 tickets, six service sites, five teams.
- Context bar: intake period 2025-01-01 to 2026-09-23; reference snapshot 2026-09-29
  19:23 (timezone unspecified); filters for dates, priority, resolver group, service site,
  intake channel; reset; visible selection count.
- Executive KPI row: total tickets 8,000; open backlog 1,801; completed SLA met 47.2%;
  median resolution 42.7 hours; customer rating 2.96/5; reopen rate 5.2%.
- Supporting facts: completed 6,199; SLA breaches 3,274; mean resolution 55.6 hours;
  ratings responses 4,508; reopened flag count 415.
- Sections: intake/completions trend, SLA by priority, backlog age profile, open workload by
  resolver group, rating distribution, channel mix, three-item decision brief, team scorecard,
  priority action queue with CSV export, definitions/assumptions/data-quality section.
- Notable labels: "Completed = Resolved + Closed", calendar-time SLA, selected intake cohort,
  ratings exclude missing, response bias caveat, comparison not adjusted for staffing/complexity.
- The PDF depicts an HTML-style interactive layout but a PDF alone DOES NOT prove filters work,
  local/offline execution works, or exports are available. Verify those separately.
- Julius reports 1,801 overdue open tickets and 1,573 older than 90 days at an inferred snapshot.
  This depends on the as-of timestamp and pause-calendar SLA assumptions; MUST NOT become a
  default assertion for other data. Inferred snapshot date must be disclosed and confirmable.

JULIUS-PLUS DELTAS (ALL REQUIRED FOR TARGET QUALITY)
A. Explainable semantic mapping, confidence, alternate interpretations, user confirmation.
B. No-AI rule-based management insight generator with numerical evidence links.
C. All three output types generated from identical immutable report model.
D. Formula lineage, metric denominators, time grain and validation statuses visible.
E. Fully responsive, keyboard-accessible, themeable, printable UI.
F. Cross-filter interactions, drill-down, saved local views, undo/reset, export current view.
G. Stronger data-quality center: missingness, duplicates, schema drift, ambiguous dates,
   nonfinite numbers, outliers, mismatched currencies/units and join-fanout hazards.
H. A configurable action queue and thresholds, never made-up targets or risk scoring.
I. Offline-safe single-file HTML with embedded data/assets and CSP-aware security.
J. Excel-native charts/tables where feasible, not merely raster images; parity tests.
K. Independent cross-export reconciliation and explicit NOT VERIFIED / BLOCKED statuses.
L. Domain-neutral fallback when no business dictionary applies.


==============================================================================================
02. USER JOURNEYS, SCREEN MAP AND PRODUCT EXPERIENCE
==============================================================================================
PRIMARY FLOW
1. Landing: privacy promise with verifiable details, sample datasets, upload CTA.
2. Drop CSV/XLSX; local parse with progress, cancellation and limits.
3. Source inspector: file type, sheet/table selector, row/column count, sample rows,
   inferred header, type badges, errors, data quality and raw-column preview.
4. Semantic review: field roles and confidence; user confirms ambiguities; no guess-to-green.
5. Dashboard planner: proposed KPI cards, sections, charts, calculations and reasons.
6. Generate: deterministic workers compute canonical aggregates; render premium dashboard.
7. Explore: date/segment filters, cross-filter, drill-through, metric tooltips, theme/layout.
8. Validate: audit center, provenance graph, source vs derived reconciliation.
9. Export: Excel / PDF / standalone HTML; options for full data, filtered view, PII inclusion,
   formula mode, paper size, theme, local encryption where supported.
10. Save report definition to local IndexedDB ONLY after explicit consent; allow clear/export.

NAVIGATION
- Workspace / Data / Field Mapping / Dashboard / Insights / Action Queue / Quality & Audit /
  Export / Saved Views / Settings.
- Responsive sticky filter ribbon and quick "Explain metric" side panel.
- User may edit report title, logo, accent, grid density and section order; edits affect all
  exports through a shared report specification, not separate ad-hoc copies.
- First-time tour may be dismissed; no mandatory account to generate a local report.

UX STATES
Idle, importing, parsing, profiling, mapping-needs-confirmation, computing, rendering,
ready, partially-supported, validation-failed, export-generating, export-complete, cancelled.
Progress indicators report real worker events, not fabricated percentages.
Error messages include affected source column/row count, cause and repair suggestion.
A safe generic dashboard is preferable to blocking all work when only business semantics
are uncertain; show confirmed summaries and ask for mapping before domain-specific metrics.


==============================================================================================
03. DATA CONTRACTS AND IMMUTABLE CANONICAL REPORT MODEL
==============================================================================================
DEFINE STRONGLY TYPED SCHEMAS; validate at boundaries (e.g. Zod or equivalent):
SourceManifest {sourceId, fileNameLocalOnly, byteLength, contentHashLocal, parserVersion,
 sheets[], rowCount, columnCount, importedAt, timeZoneAssumption?, provenanceWarnings[]}
FieldProfile {fieldId, originalHeader, displayHeader, physicalType, logicalType, nullable,
 missingCount, uniqueCount, sampleDistinct, min, max, quantiles, semanticCandidates[],
 units?, currency?, parseErrors[], sourceCoordinates, confidence, ambiguityFlags[]}
DatasetGraph {tables[], relationships[], joinCardinality, verifiedGrain?, joinRisks[]}
SemanticRole {fieldId, role, confidence, positiveEvidence[], negativeEvidence[],
 ruleId, requiresConfirmation, confirmedByUser, confirmedAt?}
MetricSpec {metricId, title, domain, description, numerator, denominator, populationFilter,
 nullPolicy, zeroDivisionPolicy, unit, format, aggregation, grain, timeBasis, ruleVersion,
 requiredRoles[], expressionAST, confidence, provenanceIds[]}
MetricResult {metricId, value, unit, sampleSize, missingExcluded, denominatorValue,
 filterFingerprint, evidenceId, status:VERIFIED|PROVISIONAL|BLOCKED|NOT_APPLICABLE}
ChartSpec {chartId, title, intent, dataQueryAST, chartType, xField, yMetrics[], segmentField?,
 sort, topN?, missingCategoryPolicy, scalePolicy, annotations[], whyChosen[], warnings[]}
InsightSpec {insightId, severity, headline, evidenceMetricIds[], comparisonBasis,
 thresholdSource, conditionsAST, recommendedReview, caveats[], ruleVersion}
ActionItem {sourceRowIds[], priorityRuleId, rankingScoreComponents[], label, reason,
 dueAsOf?, statusSourceField?, sourceFieldReferences[], isInferred}
ReportSpec {version, manifestId, semanticMap, metricSpecs, chartSpecs, filterSpec,
 dashboardSections, theme, insights, actionQueueSpec, auditSummary, exportOptions}
ExportManifest {format, reportSpecHash, sourceHash, metricResultHash, filterFingerprint,
 exportTimestamp, rendererVersion, validationEvidenceIds[], warnings[], outputHash}

HARD RULES
- Source rows are immutable and indexed by internal row IDs; preserve original column names.
- ReportSpec is the SINGLE source of truth; all exporters consume the same materialized
  validated aggregates, not separate business calculations.
- No string concatenation to execute expressions; use a typed AST and allowlisted functions.
- Store null/invalid/missing distinctly; never replace blanks with zero by default.
- Preserve precision internally; round only at presentation boundaries.
- Track dataset-local timezone assumptions; distinguish naive local dates vs UTC instants.
- No synthetic values or new columns unless explicitly marked DERIVED with expression lineage.


==============================================================================================
04. IMPORT, PARSING, TYPE PROFILING AND MULTI-TABLE SUPPORT
==============================================================================================
P0 formats: CSV (UTF-8 BOM, common encodings with confirmation, quoted delimiters), XLSX.
P1: TSV, XLS, JSON arrays/JSONL and ODS only if safe, vetted browser parsers available.
P2: Parquet/Arrow, ZIP batch import after decompression-bomb guard; no silent promise.
Enforce configured maximum upload size (currently ~50 MB) and independently enforce row,
column, cell length, workbook sheets, inflated ZIP size, formula, memory and worker-time limits.
If data exceeds device capability, explain limits; never silently sample for final metrics.

Parsing checks: MIME/signature sniffing; CSV delimiter, line ending, quote and encoding;
header detection with explicit override; multi-row headers; merged XLSX headers; blank and
hidden sheets; Excel date serial system 1900/1904; leading zeros in IDs; booleans; locale
thousands/decimal separators; scientific notation; percentages; currencies; duplicate names;
formula cached value vs formula expression; error cells; hyperlinks and unsafe strings.

Profiles: type distribution, distinct ratio, min/max, missing, skew, quantiles, date range,
invalid examples, ID patterns, monotonicity, domain candidate and unit consistency.
Rows with errors must remain traceable, with optional exclude/repair decisions logged.
Detect sheet relationships with exact key overlaps and uniqueness; reject ambiguous joins,
fanout, duplicate dimension keys and many-to-many without aggregation plan.
No joins are performed just because two columns have similar names.

Browser resource safety: use Web Workers and transferable buffers where practical, chunked
parsing, streaming aggregation, cancellation via AbortController/worker messages, memory
budget estimates, safe workbook decompression, guard against prototype pollution and
formula injection. Data files are untrusted input.


==============================================================================================
05. ZERO-AI SEMANTIC INTELLIGENCE ENGINE
==============================================================================================
IMPLEMENT A DETERMINISTIC, VERSIONED RULE ENGINE. NO LLM, EMBEDDINGS, MODEL INFERENCE,
REMOTE CLASSIFICATION, NETWORK LOOKUPS OR GENERATED BUSINESS FACTS.

A. Normalize labels: Unicode normalization, camel/snake/kebab splitting, punctuation,
   acronym dictionary, locale aliases and pluralization. Keep original labels unchanged.
B. Physical/logical types: numeric, integer, decimal, date, datetime, duration, boolean,
   category, ID, free text, monetary, percentage, geospatial candidate.
C. Semantic role candidates: ticket_id, created_at, completed_at, status, priority,
   assignee/team, site, channel, SLA_limit, reopened, rating; order_id, order_date,
   amount, cost, quantity, unit_price, discount; employee_id, join_date, leave_date;
   SKU, stock_on_hand, reorder_point; student_id, exam_score, attendance, etc.
D. Evidence scoring: header tokens, value patterns, cardinality, plausible range, field
   co-occurrence, business unit, candidate negative evidence and cross-field consistency.
E. Domain detection is MULTI-LABEL and defeasible: helpdesk, sales, HR, inventory,
   finance, marketing, education, healthcare operations, logistics, surveys, generic.
F. Apply domain guards: sales KPI requires real financial role + valid grain and units;
   helpdesk KPI requires statuses/timestamps/SLA; do not force a default sales template.
G. Confidence thresholds (configurable): high >=0.90; medium 0.70-0.899;
   low <0.70. Thresholds are product defaults to validate empirically, not proof of
   probability calibration. Any ambiguous critical field requires user confirmation.
H. Resolve conflicts by ranked evidence; if unresolved show mapping dialog, do not invent.
I. Record user overrides with before/after evidence; propagate recalculation and audit.
J. Unknown schema => neutral dashboard: row count, missingness, category distributions,
   numeric distributions, date trend only if confirmed, no fabricated business meaning.

CROSS-DOMAIN EXAMPLES
- Helpdesk: Case Ref, Logged On, Finished At, Current State, Urgency Band, Resolver Group,
  Issue Family, Intake Method, Service Site, SLA Limit (hrs), Reopened Flag, Rating.
- Sales: Order ID, Date, Net Sales, COGS, Profit, Region, Product, Quantity; reject
  unsupported margin if cost missing.
- HR: Employee ID, Department, Start Date, Exit Date, Status; headcount requires as-of
  semantics, not sum of rows when employee history has multiple records.
- Inventory: SKU, Warehouse, Stock On Hand, Reorder Point, Snapshot Date; do not sum
  repeated snapshots as current stock.
- Survey: Response ID, Question, Scale, Response; percentages use eligible denominator.
- Financial ledger: Account, Debit, Credit, Posting Date, Currency; no currency blending.

Explain mapping UI: "Interpreted 'Finished At' as completion timestamp because values
parse as dates, occur after 'Logged On', and are blank for open states." Show confidence
and alternative interpretations. Never claim all domains are perfectly inferable.


==============================================================================================
06. METRIC ENGINE, TIME LOGIC AND FORMULA LIBRARY
==============================================================================================
ONE metric registry: typed expression AST, filter context, null handling, unit checks,
row-grain checks, denominator metadata, date basis, comparison eligibility, precision,
quality requirements, provenance and deterministic execution.

BASE OPERATIONS
countRows, countDistinct, sum, avg, weightedAvg, median, percentile, min, max, variance,
stdDev, countIf, sumIf, ratio, shareOfTotal, runningTotal, movingAverage, delta,
periodOverPeriod, cohortRetention, durationBetween, ageAsOf, SLAWithinLimit.
No zero-division: emit N/A with reason. No divide-by-blank. No implicit cross-currency sums.

HELPDESK METRICS FOR FIXTURE (EXACT)
- Total tickets = COUNT(rows) = 8,000.
- Completed = count status in {Resolved, Closed} AND valid Finished At = 6,199;
  verify status/timestamp consistency and surface conflicts.
- Open = 8,000 - completed = 1,801 for this fixture; generally use explicit status map.
- Completed resolution hours = (Finished At - Logged On) / 3600, completed valid only.
- Mean resolution hours = 55.6082 (display 55.61).
- Median resolution = independently calculate from CSV; Julius displays 42.7h; do not
  hardcode this value as reference until verified by test computation.
- Completed SLA met = count completed with duration <= SLA Limit = 2,925.
- Completed SLA breached = count completed with duration > SLA Limit = 3,274.
- SLA compliance = 2,925/6,199*100 = 47.185% (display 47.19% or 47.2%).
- Reopened = 415 flagged. Julius shows 5.2% = 415/8,000; existing reference JSON
  describes "reopen_rate = sum flag / resolved tickets" (=6.69%). THESE ARE DIFFERENT
  DEFINITIONS. Never label either generically as "Reopen Rate" without denominator.
  Offer: "Reopened share of all tickets" 415/8000=5.1875% and "Reopened share of
  completed tickets" 415/6199≈6.69%, with clear cohort qualification. If flags appear
  on open tickets, do not imply 415 are completed reopened tickets.
- Rated responses = 4,508; mean customer rating = 2.9627 / 5 (display 2.96).
- Rating response rate = 4,508 / eligible completed tickets only if eligibility
  established; Julius uses 72.7%=4508/6199, but this requires documenting whether
  open tickets with ratings exist. Validate population before labeling coverage.
- Backlog age as of reference date requires an EXPLICIT as-of date and timezone.
  Don't infer from current date or newest row without user-visible confirmation.
- SLA for OPEN tickets: overdue calendar-time is NOT the same as completed SLA breach;
  do not assert 100% overdue absent valid snapshot, pause calendar and policy.
- Trends: intake by Logged On month; completions by Finished At month, clearly note
  selected intake cohort vs total completed in calendar period.

FORMULA SEMANTICS
- Duration uses timestamps and timezone handling; negative/invalid durations excluded
  with error counts; distinguish business hours vs calendar hours.
- Percentages label numerator, denominator, inclusion/exclusion and date context.
- Missing rating excluded from average; missing completion time never zero duration.
- Percentile and median algorithm documented; tie/quantile method consistent everywhere.
- Financial metrics enforce sign conventions, unit consistency, discounts/tax definitions.
- Derived columns never overwrite raw source columns.
- Comparisons (YoY, MoM) require comparable periods, coverage and minimum observations.
- Avoid meaningless averages of rates; use weighted numerator/denominator aggregation.


==============================================================================================
07. PREMIUM JULIUS-PLUS DASHBOARD COMPOSITION
==============================================================================================
LAYOUT: executive visual language, crisp type scale, restrained color palette, high contrast,
white space, compact readable scorecards, optional dark mode, consistent chart legends.

SECTION A: Context / filters
- Dataset title, record count, period, as-of date, domain, confidence, report timestamp.
- Responsive filter bar: date range, categories, groups, sites, priorities, source table.
- Filter chips, clear all, reset, saved views, URL-independent local view state.
- Display N selected of total; warn when chart/metric uses different eligible cohort.

SECTION B: Executive KPI strip
- 4-8 cards chosen by domain and available valid metrics, NOT fixed to six.
- Value, unit, denominator subtitle, optional change with comparable prior period,
  tooltip with formula, source, confidence and exclusion counts.
- Do not show trend arrows when no valid comparator.

SECTION C: Demand & delivery
- Timeline combo charts (intake/completions), SLA by priority or process stage;
  show incomplete months and different cohort semantics.

SECTION D: Backlog and risk
- Age buckets, status distribution, team-by-priority stacked bars, thresholds and
  caveats; only if dates, statuses and as-of context are valid.

SECTION E: Experience & channels
- Rating distribution, satisfaction coverage, intake channel mix, missing responses.

SECTION F: Decision brief
- 3-6 ranked, evidence-linked deterministic insight cards, user-configurable thresholds,
  "what this means", "what to verify", "suggested next action".

SECTION G: Scorecards
- Group/site/category table with counts, denominators, medians, quality flags, sort,
  search, pagination and export; comparisons labeled descriptive, not causal.

SECTION H: Action queue
- Top N actionable source rows ranked by transparent rules, user-selected priority,
  open age, breach risk, exceptions; click to inspect original row and evidence.

SECTION I: Data quality and methodology
- Field mapping, assumptions, missingness, duplicate counts, reconciliation, export
  evidence, formula dictionary and caveats. Available in all formats.

ADVANCED FEATURES
- Drill-down and drill-through with breadcrumbs; cross-highlighting; brush-select;
  chart swapping; annotations; saved local filters; user-built calculated measures using
  safe AST editor; date grain controls; unit formatting; compact/expanded layouts.
- Responsive breakpoints; no cut-off legends, charts or table cells in exports.
- Show "No meaningful chart" when dimensionality or sample size is unsuitable.


==============================================================================================
08. AUTONOMOUS CHART RECOMMENDATION ENGINE
==============================================================================================
Select charts based on data shape, semantic role, statistical suitability and audience.
- Time + metric => line/area; sparse periods => columns; explain missing periods.
- Category + count => sorted horizontal bar; high cardinality => top N + Other.
- Category + rate => bar with denominator and optional Wilson confidence interval
  only when statistically appropriate and correctly implemented.
- Part-to-whole => donut only for few nonnegative mutually exclusive categories;
  otherwise bar/treemap. Do not use donut for arbitrary continuous measures.
- Priority x team => stacked bar or heatmap; warn if color implies misleading ranking.
- Distribution => histogram, box plot, quantile strip; require enough observations.
- Numeric x numeric => scatter with outlier handling and sample size note.
- Cohort => retention matrix only with real cohort IDs/time semantics.
- Geographical maps only with reliable geocoding and explicit user consent; default to
  bar charts for site names, never invent coordinates.
- Choose zero baseline for ordinary bar charts; prevent misleading truncation.
- Reject unsupported combinations; chart selection must include reason and rejected
  alternatives. All chart queries reuse canonical aggregates.
- Target 5-9 meaningful visuals for rich datasets; fewer when data does not support them.
- Avoid filler visuals; adapt to screen size and export pagination.


==============================================================================================
09. DETERMINISTIC MANAGEMENT INSIGHTS AND ACTION ENGINE
==============================================================================================
NO GENERATIVE AI. Insight engine = library of explicit conditions, thresholds,
comparators, ranking and text templates with bound numeric values.

Insight rule contract:
{ruleId, version, applicableDomain, requiredMetrics, eligibility, conditionAST,
 severity, template, evidenceRefs, counterexamples, caveatTemplate, thresholdSource}

EXAMPLES
- "X of Y completed tickets breached SLA (Z%). Review SLA definitions and routing."
- "N high-priority tickets remain open as of DATE. Review oldest first."
- "Ratings are missing for M of E eligible tickets; avoid overgeneralizing the mean."
- "Team completion rates vary by P percentage points; compare priority mix before
   attributing cause."
- "Metric increased X% vs comparable previous period" ONLY if time coverage valid.

RULE SAFETY
- No unsupported causal claims, guarantees, prescriptive medical/financial advice,
  invented targets, inferred urgency beyond configured threshold, fabricated sentiment.
- Every statement links to computed metric IDs and source row populations.
- Thresholds are tagged USER_CONFIGURED, DOMAIN_DEFAULT_WITH_SOURCE, or NONE;
  generic arbitrary percentages cannot be marketed as universal standards.
- Insight priorities: verified risk > data-quality issues > notable changes > descriptive.
- Deduplicate contradictory or overlapping cards; cap cards; display "no validated
  insights" when evidence insufficient.
- Action queue is a filtered/sorted view of actual records; no fictitious tasks.
- Explain priority score components and ties; allow user adjustment and export.


==============================================================================================
10. INTERACTIVITY, FILTER ENGINE AND PERFORMANCE
==============================================================================================
FilterSpec typed expression tree: AND/OR, include/exclude, range, relative date,
null category, search, numeric threshold. No eval and no arbitrary JS execution.
- Filter propagation across cards/charts/tables/insights/action queue.
- Cross-filter selected segment; clearly show selected N and denominator changes.
- Cache aggregates keyed by source hash + semantic version + filter fingerprint.
- Avoid re-parsing on filter changes; use typed arrays/indexes/columnar memory as useful.
- Worker execution for heavy aggregates, percentiles, PDF rendering and XLSX packaging.
- UI remains responsive; cancellation; yield to browser; memory release on reset.
- Measure import, profile, map, compute, render, export separately via performance marks
  kept local and optionally downloadable as diagnostic JSON.

PERFORMANCE TARGETS (GOALS, NOT CURRENT FACTS)
- 8k x 12 Helpdesk CSV: preview <=2s, core metrics <=5s, interactive dashboard <=10s,
  Excel/PDF/HTML each ideally <=20s on a documented reference Windows desktop.
- 50MB input: no unbounded heap, graceful memory/device warnings, progress, cancel.
- No universal 20-second guarantee without browser/device/file complexity benchmark.
- Run repeated warm/cold benchmarks on Chrome, Edge, Firefox; document p50/p95 and
  peak memory. Never optimize speed by skipping validation or silently sampling.


==============================================================================================
11. EXPORT ENGINE — ONE SOURCE, THREE PROFESSIONAL FORMATS
==============================================================================================
EXPORT PRECONDITION
Canonical ReportSpec + materialized validated aggregates + AuditSummary.
Every export records source hash, filter fingerprint, metric hash, semantic rule versions,
format renderer version and warnings. Critical audit failures block "verified" labeling;
allow explicitly marked DRAFT export only if user acknowledges.

A. EXCEL .XLSX
- Native editable workbook; summary/dashboard sheet, supporting analysis sheets, original
  source table (subject to Excel row limits), methodology, evidence and data-quality.
- Use real Excel tables and native chart objects where library capability allows;
  avoid raster-only dashboards. Preserve formulas for auditable KPIs where safe and
  performant; cached results/Excel recalculation flags must be tested in Excel desktop.
- Where native formulas/charts are not supported, clearly disclose limitations and
  provide static values with full formula definitions in methodology.
- Avoid external workbook links, macros, untrusted formulas and formula injection.
- Formula injection guard for source strings beginning =,+,-,@ in CSV-derived cells;
  preserve original text semantics without execution.
- Workbook styling: merged title areas where appropriate, frozen headers, number formats,
  conditional formatting, readable widths, print areas, sensible sheet names <=31 chars,
  accessible palettes, no overlapping charts.
- Excel interoperability: Microsoft Excel desktop and LibreOffice visual and numeric
  checks; do not claim full recalculation safe until executed test evidence exists.
- Excel exports must not invent domain-specific sheets; Helpdesk must not get Sales
  Transactions/Product Catalog/Net Revenue/Software Margin.

B. PDF .PDF
- A4/Letter and landscape options, vector text and vector charts when feasible,
  embedded/standard fonts, crisp print resolution, page numbers, repeated table headers,
  appropriate section page breaks, no truncated legends or overlapping content.
- Include executive summary, filters/time basis, methodology, provenance, quality caveats;
  configurable appendix of detailed records. Pagination adapts to dataset complexity.
- Optional high-resolution PNG/SVG chart assets generated locally, without CDN.
- PDF is a static snapshot: do not falsely label interactive filters as operational.
- Verify PDF visually at 100% and 200% zoom, text selectable, contrast and pagination.

C. HTML .HTML
- Download standalone single-file report with embedded JS/CSS/data/chart engine (or
  packaged offline ZIP only if single-file exceeds safe limits and user explicitly chooses).
- No network dependencies: no CDN, Google Fonts, remote telemetry, analytics, image fetch,
  external iframes, API calls, service-worker registrations or remote assets.
- Safe serialization and HTML escaping; strict local security policy compatible with
  file:// usage, no eval, no unsanitized innerHTML, no executable source-cell markup.
- Filterable, keyboard accessible, responsive, printable, local CSV export for selected
  records, reset, legends and tooltips. Data embedded means recipient can inspect it;
  warn user before embedding sensitive rows or identifiers.
- Offer aggregate-only HTML privacy option, excluding raw rows and PII.
- Browser compatibility: Chrome/Edge/Firefox local file mode; verify offline with
  network disabled and no fetch requests.

EXPORT PARITY
- Shared metric values identical at full precision, same filter population, same chart
  categories and same caveats across Excel/PDF/HTML; allow documented format-specific
  interaction differences. Snapshot hash embedded in each artifact.
- Export button disabled only while needed, with clear failure recovery; don't silently
  produce partial/corrupt downloads.


==============================================================================================
12. PRIVACY, SECURITY AND VERIFIABLE ZERO-CLOUD CLAIM
==============================================================================================
ZERO-AI: No LLM calls, model APIs, remote embeddings, AI SDKs or hosted analytics for
user content. Static rule dictionaries ship with application assets.
ZERO DATA UPLOAD: Never send dataset bytes, rows, derived values, column names, file names,
chart snapshots, error excerpts or report contents to server or third parties.
- Implement Content Security Policy, Trusted Types where supported, safe DOM rendering,
  dependency pinning, supply-chain scanning, strict MIME/ZIP checks and sanitized downloads.
- Optional website analytics may collect only non-sensitive aggregate site events with
  consent and must be isolated from workspace; no data-derived dimensions.
- Error handling must redact dataset values, filenames and field names from remote logs.
- Local IndexedDB storage opt-in, explain retention, delete-all control and browser limits.
- No silent remote font, CDN script, image, or browser reporting endpoint from exported HTML.
- Audit using browser DevTools network capture with sample sensitive marker values;
  inspect requests, crash reporting and console logs under import/filter/export.
- Threat tests: CSV formula injection, XLSX zip bombs, malicious cell strings, XSS,
  giant numbers, unsafe dates, deeply nested JSON, path traversal, resource exhaustion,
  compromised dependencies, service-worker cache contamination.
- Be precise in marketing: "processed locally in your browser" after verification;
  "offline-capable" only after standalone report and optional app-shell offline tests.


==============================================================================================
13. VALIDATION, AUDIT, EVIDENCE AND TRUST STATES
==============================================================================================
AUDIT STATES: VERIFIED, PARTIALLY_VERIFIED, NEEDS_CONFIRMATION, FAILED, NOT_TESTED.
Never print 100% RECONCILED, CONFIRMED GRAIN, SEALED & AUDITED, CURRENCY PURE or
FULL RECALC SAFE without corresponding independent executed evidence.

VALIDATION LAYERS
1. Parse: rows/columns, errors, source-preservation and byte/hash lineage.
2. Schema: inferred types, null counts, distinct IDs, negative durations, duplicates.
3. Semantics: roles, domain guards, confidence and user overrides.
4. Calculation: independent reference engine, exact count reconciliation, tolerance
   for floating point, denominator/units and group sum invariants.
5. Charts: data values, totals, categories, scale, filtering and title accuracy.
6. Insights: bound metrics, correct thresholds, caveats and evidence references.
7. Exports: Excel/PDF/HTML numeric parity, page integrity, no domain hallucination.
8. Privacy: network trace contains no source data or derived values.

REQUIRED AUDIT OUTPUT
- Source hash; import and parser versions; row counts and exclusions.
- Semantic map with evidence and confirmed/uncertain status.
- Metric dictionary, formula AST, population, numerator/denominator, precision, units.
- Reconciliation table with observed, expected, delta, tolerance, pass/fail.
- Chart and insight provenance; date and as-of assumptions.
- Export renderer versions and format-specific caveats.
- Privacy self-test outcome and tests actually run (not implied).
- User can export audit JSON locally and open metric lineage inspector.

INDEPENDENCE
Validator must not merely re-read cached KPI results from the generator. Use separate
reference implementations or property-based invariants for crucial totals, plus fixed
fixture golden outputs. Avoid both engines sharing the same buggy aggregation helper.


==============================================================================================
14. QUALITY GATES AND CROSS-DOMAIN TEST MATRIX
==============================================================================================
P0 FIXTURE A: 8,000-row Helpdesk CSV (attached in this conversation)
Golden expected: total 8000, completed 6199, open 1801, SLA met 2925, breached 3274,
SLA compliance 47.185%, mean completed resolution 55.6082h, rating responses 4508,
mean rating 2.9627, reopened flags 415, channel Portal 3493, Email 2220,
Phone 1223, Chat 1064, five resolver groups.
- Validate reopened denominator ambiguity explicitly: 5.1875% all-ticket share vs
  415/6199 completed denominator (not interchangeable).
- No revenue/cost/margin/order/customer/SKU sheets or metrics.
- As-of-dependent overdue claims only when user confirms snapshot/policy.
- Visuals: operational metrics, trends, SLA, backlog, teams, ratings, channels.
- Generate Excel/PDF/HTML and compare displayed KPI results and source counts.

P0 FIXTURE B: 6,000-row Sales MIS CSV
Golden expected: quantity 17,966; Net Sales INR 339,830,586.55;
COGS INR 267,920,146.50; Gross Profit INR 71,910,440.05; margin 21.16%.
- Retain correct sales functionality while preventing fake targets and invented YoY.

P0 FIXTURE C: arbitrary unknown fields
- Example X17, V_2, ZQ; neutral statistical report; request mapping for business KPIs.

P1 FIXTURES: HR employee snapshots vs history, inventory daily stock, finance ledger
multi-currency, survey with missing answers, education scores, logistics timestamps,
marketing funnel with multiple grains, healthcare operations without clinical inference.

TEST CATEGORIES (MINIMUM 100 AUTOMATED CASES; EXPAND EXISTING v6 80 CASES)
01-10 parsing: CSV delimiters/encoding/quotes/BOM; XLSX multi-sheet; blank sheets;
       Excel serials; duplicate headers; hidden rows; invalid files; 50MB guard.
11-20 typing: IDs with zeros; currencies; percent units; mixed dates; locale;
       nulls; invalid durations; booleans; ambiguous headers; scientific notation.
21-30 semantic: Helpdesk recognition; Sales recognition; mixed domains; unknown
       fields; negative evidence; ambiguous role; confidence override; no invented KPI.
31-40 grain/joins: duplicate keys; composite keys; dimension uniqueness; fanout;
       many-to-many; conflicting join; snapshots; slowly changing dimensions; null IDs.
41-50 metrics: exact counts; distinct; missing exclusions; mean/median; weighted
       rates; zero denominators; unit mismatches; period alignment; SLA boundaries.
51-60 filtering: multiple filters; null bucket; date range; reset; cross-filter;
       selected denominator; drill-through; date grain; saved view; export filtered.
61-70 chart: valid type selection; rejected unsuitable type; topN+Other; labels;
       empty chart; outlier scale; category totals; date gaps; legends; accessibility.
71-80 insights/actions: threshold source; evidence link; no invented targets;
       deduplication; missing data caveat; as-of confirmation; action ranking;
       source row trace; zero-result state; no unsupported causality.
81-90 exports: native Excel charts; Excel formula injection; PDF page breaks;
       text selectable; standalone HTML offline; HTML XSS; parity hash;
       large file warnings; no clipped labels; multi-browser compatibility.
91-100 security/performance: no network upload; no telemetry leak; zip bomb;
       cancellation; worker memory; no long UI block; CSP; local persistence delete;
       p95 benchmarks; export recovery.
101-110 regression: all 32 templates still selectable; no template forced;
       original source preservation; workbook audit honesty; locale; timezone;
       comparison periods; reopened denominator; no duplicated rows; crash restart.

AUTOMATED EVIDENCE
- Unit tests, property-based tests, snapshot/golden fixtures, export OOXML assertions,
  independent CSV reference script, PDF page render comparison, browser E2E,
  privacy network interception and cross-browser download validation.
- Tests must fail CI if helpdesk gets finance labels or a false verified audit badge.


==============================================================================================
15. PHASED IMPLEMENTATION, FILE STRUCTURE AND ACCEPTANCE
==============================================================================================
PHASE 0 — REPOSITORY AUDIT (NO FEATURE CHANGES)
Inventory current stack, 32 templates, parser, existing Excel exporter, all calculations,
server endpoints, telemetry, data-flow diagram and known issues. Output dependency map,
risks and compatibility plan. Ask owner before destructive migrations.

PHASE 1 — P0 SEMANTIC SAFETY
Build typed dataset manifest, immutable rows, semantic candidates and domain guards.
Fix Helpdesk mapping, remove fabricated financial values and false audit assertions.
Pass 8k Helpdesk and 6k Sales fixtures. BLOCK further polish until passing.

PHASE 2 — CANONICAL CALCULATION + INDEPENDENT AUDIT
Implement metric registry, AST, filter context, provenance, separate validator,
source/group invariants, missingness and explicit as-of logic.

PHASE 3 — JULIUS-PLUS RESPONSIVE DASHBOARD
Implement dashboard planner, premium layout, sections A-I, cross-filters, scorecards,
insights, action queue, explanations and data-quality center.

PHASE 4 — THREE EXPORTERS
Excel native objects and formulas where supported; print-quality PDF; standalone
offline HTML. All consume identical ReportSpec and validated materialized results.

PHASE 5 — PRIVACY, PERFORMANCE, ACCESSIBILITY, CROSS-BROWSER
Network leakage test, workers, cancellation, 50MB safety, keyboard/screen reader,
local file HTML, browser matrix and p95 benchmark.

PHASE 6 — PILOT, LAUNCH AND OBSERVABILITY
10+ testers across domains and devices. Collect only explicitly volunteered test
feedback; no sensitive file contents. Fix defects; release notes and rollback plan.

SUGGESTED LOGICAL MODULES (ADAPT TO REPO, DO NOT FORCE REWRITE)
/src/engine/import, profile, semantic, graph, metrics, filters, charts, insights,
 actions, validation, report-spec, provenance
/src/export/excel, pdf, html, shared-render-model
/src/workers/import.worker, compute.worker, export.worker
/src/components/workspace, mapping, dashboard, scorecard, chart, action-queue,
 quality, audit, export-dialog
/tests/fixtures, unit, property, e2e, golden, security, privacy, performance

DEFINITION OF DONE
[ ] Helpdesk report correct, domain appropriate and source-reconciled.
[ ] Sales report correct and existing features/templates preserved.
[ ] Unknown dataset handled honestly without fabricated semantics.
[ ] All 3 exports produced locally, professionally formatted, numerically identical.
[ ] Audit statuses only reflect executed evidence; metric lineage visible.
[ ] No source data sent over network during upload, analysis, filters or exports.
[ ] HTML works offline with no external dependencies; Excel opens without repair;
    PDF readable and unclipped.
[ ] 110+ tests pass; P0/P1 issues resolved; measured performance documented.
[ ] No claims of universal understanding, guaranteed 20 seconds or 100% verified
    unless actual test evidence supports the exact claim.

IMPLEMENTATION INSTRUCTIONS FOR CODEX / ANTIGRAVITY
1. Read THIS WHOLE DOCUMENT including preserved v6 requirements below.
2. Audit current repo first; show implementation plan and affected files.
3. Implement in small reversible commits with tests and no unrelated refactoring.
4. Do not substitute AI, hosted APIs, server-side parsing, analytics or upload services.
5. Do not bypass semantic/validation gates to make demo look successful.
6. Maintain a change log, fixture results, benchmark and remaining known limitations.
7. Ask for approval before deploying, changing hosting/database or introducing costs.
8. Demonstrate 8k Helpdesk and 6k Sales in all three formats with independent audit.


==============================================================================================
16. SOURCE LIMITATIONS, CONFLICT RESOLUTION AND PRIORITY OF REQUIREMENTS
==============================================================================================
- This v7 addendum is authoritative over conflicting v6 requirements, especially the
  prohibition on optional AI and the clarified reopened-rate denominator.
- The user-provided Julius PDF is an example of layout and report content, not a
  verified description of Julius product implementation, privacy, export availability,
  actual interactivity or benchmark time.
- The 8k Helpdesk and 6k Sales reference JSONs are fixture expectations; where their
  labels/denominators are ambiguous, show both definitions and require explicit choice.
- Every item in this document is a requested target, not a claim that the current app
  already implements it.
- If constraints conflict (e.g. 50MB input and all raw rows embedded in HTML), choose
  privacy/safety and explicit user selection over silent data loss or unsafe output.
- If a feature is not supported by the selected browser XLSX/PDF library, document the
  exact gap and implement a tested fallback without pretending it is native/editable.


