- Uploaded second export: Helpdesk_Operations_8000_Tickets_Universal_Dashboard_Test_Upload_Executive_Sales_Dashboard_2026.xlsx.
- Workbook contains sheets Executive Dashboard, Pivot Table Analysis, Sales Transactions, Regional Performance, Product Catalog, Customer Accounts, Audit & Data Provenance.
- Stored shared-string text contains 'Blended Software Margin', 'Net Revenue', '100% RECONCILED', 'CONFIRMED GRAIN', 'CURRENCY PURE', 'SEALED & AUDITED', 'FULL RECALC SAFE'.
- These are concrete export findings; XML presence alone does not prove formula recalculation or that each visual is currently visible. Perform Excel desktop verification.
- This dataset has no finance fields. Such labels are semantically inappropriate. Any audit badge asserting verification requires evidence from independently executed validation.

B. RELEASE BLOCKERS (P0) — IMPLEMENT FIRST
B01. Domain guard: unsupported semantic mappings cause BLOCKED_METRIC, not synthetic substitutes.
B02. Provenance gate: audit sheet must never show PASS without actual executed test results and evidence ID.
B03. Source fidelity: every source row and original value preserved; no timestamp mapped to customer ID.
B04. Zero invented fields: no revenue, cost, margin, currency, product SKU, customer tier, region manager, sales target or software licenses for helpdesk.
B05. Dataset-specific dashboard: generate helpdesk operational pages and KPIs, not sales.
B06. Row grain verified from keys, not asserted from the chosen template.
B07. Independent reconciliation: totals and subgroup counts must match raw uploaded source.
B08. Explicit uncertainty: if domain unclear, neutral profile and mapping confirmation, never confident but wrong sales report.
B09. Fix audit metadata: unsupported certification wording removed until gates pass.
B10. No regressions on correct 6,000-row Sales MIS fixture.

C. IMPLEMENTATION CONTRACTS (TYPES / INTERFACES)
Use equivalent strongly typed interfaces in the actual codebase; do not force a language rewrite.
DatasetManifest:
 {sourceId, filename, sha256, mimeDetected, sizeBytes, importedAt, parserVersion,
  sheets:[{name, selectedRanges, headerRow, rowCount, columnCount, hidden, mergedRanges}],
  privacyMode, warnings}
ColumnProfile:
 {columnId, originalHeader, normalizedHeader, originalIndex, physicalType,
  logicalType, semanticCandidates:[{role, confidence, evidence, contraindications}],
  nullable, missingCount, missingRate, distinctCount, distinctRate,
  min, max, mean, median, quantiles, topValues, lengthStats, examplesRedacted,
  parseSuccessRate, unit, currency, timezone, ambiguityFlags, sensitivity}
TableProfile:
 {tableId, rowCount, keyCandidates, grainCandidates, primaryKey, grainConfidence,
  dateCandidates, measureCandidates, dimensions, qualityIssues, rowHashSamples}
Relationship:
 {leftTable, leftKey, rightTable, rightKey, cardinality, matchedRate,
  nullKeyRate, duplicateKeyRate, inferredConfidence, fanoutRisk, enabled}
SemanticDecision:
 {sourceColumnIds, targetRole, status: confirmed|inferred|ambiguous|rejected,
  confidence, evidence, conflicts, requiresConfirmation, userDecision, version}
MetricDefinition:
 {id, label, domain, eligibleGrains, requiredRoles, requiredUnits, filters,
  aggregation, numerator, denominator, nullPolicy, zeroPolicy, timePolicy,
  currencyPolicy, expectedRange, resultType, displayFormat, lineage}
MetricExecution:
 {metricId, inputRows, includedRows, excludedRows, value, warnings,
  numeratorValue, denominatorValue, sourceHash, executionVersion}
ChartSpec:
 {chartId, purpose, metricIds, dimensionIds, chartType, aggregation,
  filterContext, sorting, topN, otherGroup, axisUnits, seriesLimit,
  dataPoints, warnings, validationStatus}
ValidationEvidence:
 {checkId, severity, expected, actual, tolerance, algorithm, sourceHash,
  timestamp, status: pass|fail|not_run|not_applicable, affectedMetrics, evidencePath}
ExportManifest:
 {sourceHash, schemaVersion, mappingVersion, metricEngineVersion, chartEngineVersion,
  workbookVersion, includedSheets, checkResults, verificationStatus}
Every field must be serializable, versioned and testable. Do not put raw PII into telemetry.

D. FILE DISCOVERY AND STRUCTURE (MUST HANDLE REAL-WORLD FILES)
D01 CSV: UTF-8 with/without BOM, supported legacy encodings with detection warning, separators comma/semicolon/tab/pipe, quoting/escaped delimiters, embedded newlines, locale-specific decimal separators, inconsistent rows, repeated header lines.
D02 XLSX: multiple sheets, hidden sheets, merged cells, multi-row headings, title rows above table, Excel Tables, formulas vs cached results, date serials including 1900/1904 systems, named ranges, blank separators, repeated sections.
D03 Find candidate tabular blocks using density and type consistency; do not assume first row is header or first sheet is data.
D04 Detect multi-table worksheets and show selection preview. Detect totals/subtotals and exclude from row-level facts to prevent double counting.
D05 Reject or safely isolate macros, external links, unsupported formula evaluation, corrupt archives and decompression bombs.
D06 Preserve header originals, case, order, duplicates (with stable internal IDs), leading zeros, and unparsed raw cells.
D07 Parse large files incrementally; cancellation and memory budget; explicit 50 MB upload policy and workbook decompressed-size guard.
D08 Never claim 'any file' literally: accept supported tabular formats; for unsupported formats return informative error, not invented interpretation.
D09 Determine locale/timezone only from explicit metadata or user confirmation when ambiguous. Store UTC only when timezone known; preserve local original.
D10 Detect multiple plausible tables and ask which to analyze, or analyze separately without joining until verified.

E. COLUMN TYPE AND SEMANTIC INFERENCE
E01 Separate physical type (string, float, boolean, date) from logical type (ID, currency, rate, duration, status, timestamp, count, free text, geographic code).
E02 Use all nonempty values or representative stratified samples for initial inference, then validate against entire column before certifying.
E03 Numeric-looking identifiers (ZIP, SKU, phone, invoice, account IDs) must remain strings; leading zeros retained.
E04 Rate detection: 0..1 vs 0..100, `%` strings, basis points; never guess scaling silently. Ask when both plausible.
E05 Units: hours/minutes/days, kilograms/pieces, currencies, temperature, distance; reject incompatible arithmetic.
E06 Date parsing: ISO, Excel serial, D/M/Y vs M/D/Y ambiguity, timestamps, daylight-saving gaps, partial dates; keep original text.
E07 Boolean mapping: true/false, yes/no, 0/1, reopened flag; only when value distribution and context support.
E08 Status: categorical not numeric; support custom lifecycle and explicit terminal statuses; do not treat all statuses as 'Closed'.
E09 Free text: distinguish comments/descriptions from category fields; avoid exposing private text in exported charts.
E10 Semantic inference evidence: header synonyms + value patterns + units + neighboring columns + dataset context + key/grain constraints.
E11 Negative evidence mandatory: 'SLA Limit (hrs)' is NOT money; 'Logged On' is NOT customer ID; 'Customer Rating' is NOT revenue.
E12 Confidence thresholds configurable, calibrated and domain-specific; no invented universal 0.9 threshold. High confidence still requires invariant checks.
E13 Ambiguous 'Amount', 'Value', 'Date', 'ID', 'Score' require clarification if their mapping changes financial or operational conclusions.
E14 If columns are renamed A,B,C, infer only safe primitive facts unless value semantics are strong; request concise confirmation.
E15 Prevent semantic leakage from filename, selected template, old session or cached mapping; user-selected template is a presentation hint, never evidence of source facts.
E16 Validate mapping after every schema change and every new upload, even if a saved profile exists.
E17 Detect categorical numeric codes (1,2,3) vs quantitative measures; avoid inappropriate sum/average.
E18 Detect percent vs fractional value from values AND header, with warnings for conflicts.

F. MISSINGNESS, QUALITY AND OUTLIERS
F01 Distinguish absent field, empty cell, whitespace, 'NA', 'N/A', 'NULL', dash, zero, negative, invalid token, redacted value.
F02 Per-column missing count/rate; per-row completeness; patterns by category/date; co-missingness correlations.
F03 Missingness can be structural: unresolved helpdesk tickets have blank Finished At by design, not necessarily bad data.
F04 Never replace blank completion time with opening time or zero duration.
F05 Null numeric values excluded from averages with coverage denominator shown; additive sums may treat missing as unknown rather than zero based on metric policy.
F06 Outliers flagged, not automatically removed. Preserve original rows and expose exclusions with reasons.
F07 Detect duplicates using candidate keys and near-duplicate content; never silently deduplicate valid line items.
F08 Quality scores must disclose formula, weightings and uncertainty; no opaque 100% badges.
F09 Data validation failures may block only affected KPIs, not the entire file when a safe generic profile is possible.
F10 Add missingness heatmap and schema report when useful; do not clutter executive page with quality visuals if no issue exists.

G. KEYS, ROW GRAIN, TABLE RELATIONSHIPS
G01 Candidate keys: uniqueness, null rate, stability, type, semantic meaning; high uniqueness alone does not prove ID.
G02 Detect event row, transaction line, transaction header, account snapshot, daily inventory snapshot, survey response, time-series observation, multi-measure aggregate.
G03 Helpdesk: Case Ref unique => candidate ticket grain; validate against source. Multiple updates per case => event grain; count DISTINCT Case Ref for tickets.
G04 Sales: order line vs order ID: distinct orders differs from number of lines; use explicit labels.
G05 Inventory: stock on hand is semi-additive across time; choose last valid period balance, not sum daily stock.
G06 Multi-sheet relationships: candidate join key overlap, referential integrity, cardinality and fanout analysis.
G07 Never auto-join when many-to-many or ambiguous keys could multiply financial totals; preview join impact.
G08 Compare pre/post join row counts and aggregate invariant metrics; block if unintended fanout.
G09 Mixed granularity columns in same sheet require distinct metric rules; preserve dimensions at their native grain.
G10 Allow user confirmation for ambiguous relationships, and show diagram + risk warnings.

H. DOMAIN-NEUTRAL METRIC ENGINE
H01 No universal 'Revenue' default. Metrics are generated from validated semantics, not template names.
H02 Metric operators: count, distinct count, sum, weighted average, min/max, median/percentile, duration difference, ratios, last observation, cohort and period comparison where eligible.
H03 Typed dimensional analysis: money cannot add to hours; ratios require numerator/denominator units; currencies cannot mix without FX.
H04 Deterministic metric registry with domain packs: sales, helpdesk, HR, inventory, education, finance, healthcare operations (nonclinical), marketing, projects, logistics, survey, generic.
H05 Each pack has required/optional roles and explicit contraindications; packs may coexist without forcing a single domain.
H06 Generic fallback: row count, missingness, unique keys, categorical distributions, numeric descriptive stats, valid time trends. No financial or operational jargon.
H07 All ratios calculated from aggregate numerators/denominators, not arithmetic mean of row ratios unless mathematically intended.
H08 Exclude missing observations from denominator only according to stated business definition. Show coverage.
H09 Define denominator-zero behavior: N/A with reason, not 0%.
H10 Preserve negative values; treat returns, refunds, cancellations and reversals based on source semantics.
H11 Avoid causal claims, forecasts, targets, anomalies and benchmarks without sufficient evidence and opt-in policy.
H12 User overrides are marked USER_DEFINED and stored in provenance; never treated as verified source values.
H13 Independent validation implementation must not share the same aggregation code path as metric execution.
H14 Calculations in UI, export and validation must share canonical metric definitions, not three inconsistent formula implementations.
H15 Decimal-safe finance, precise duration arithmetic, stable sorting, reproducible rounding and locale-aware display.

I. HELPDESK METRIC PACK — EXACT CONTRACT FOR TEST FIXTURE
I01 Source roles:
 Case Ref=case ID; Logged On=opened timestamp; Finished At=completion timestamp;
 Current State=workflow status; Urgency Band=priority; Resolver Group=team;
 Issue Family=category; Intake Method=channel; Service Site=site;
 SLA Limit (hrs)=allowed resolution hours; Reopened Flag=boolean; Customer Rating (1-5)=optional rating.
I02 Total tickets=distinct valid Case Ref if confirmed unique and one case per row; source fixture 8,000.
I03 Completed=rows with Finished At and terminal Current State Resolved/Closed; fixture 6,199.
I04 Unresolved=all tickets minus completed; fixture 1,801. Flag contradictions (closed status with missing timestamp).
I05 Resolution duration hours=(Finished At - Logged On) in elapsed hours for completed only. Negative duration invalid.
I06 SLA met=completed duration <= SLA Limit; breached=> SLA Limit; fixture met 2,925, breached 3,274.
I07 SLA compliance=met/completed valid SLA-evaluable tickets; fixture 47.185% (47.19% display).
I08 Average resolution=mean valid completed durations; fixture 55.6082 hours (55.61 display).
I09 Reopened count=SUM validated boolean flag; compute from source/reference, not remembered narrative.
I10 Rating mean=average nonblank valid 1–5 ratings only; coverage=valid rating count/eligible population as explicitly defined.
I11 Ticket trends by Logged On month; never by fabricated Order Date.
I12 Team workload, urgency mix, issue families, intake channels, sites and statuses as real categories; unknown groups explicit.
I13 Open-ticket SLA breach as-of requires a user-selected as-of timestamp and policy; otherwise omit.
I14 If SLA thresholds missing, show completion metrics but mark SLA unsupported.
I15 If priority changes over time, single-row latest status vs event history must be distinguished.
I16 Validate every metric against independent JSON fixture and source CSV; don't hardcode expected numbers in production engine.
I17 Never generate USD, margin, SKU, accounts, regional sales, customer tier or software license KPIs from these fields.

J. CHART INTELLIGENCE & COMPOSITION
J01 Visual grammar chooses by semantic role, grain, cardinality, sign, completeness, time continuity, and user goal.
J02 Timestamp+count => monthly/weekly trend; timezone and missing periods explicitly handled.
J03 Category+count => sorted bar; high cardinality => Top N + Other, totals reconcile.
J04 Two categorical dimensions => stacked bar or heatmap if cardinality manageable.
J05 Numeric distribution => histogram/boxplot; explain outlier treatment.
J06 Duration => histogram/median/p90 and grouped bars by priority/team when sample sizes sufficient.
J07 Rating 1..5 => distribution and average with response coverage; never present a rating as currency.
J08 Part-to-whole only when categories are mutually exclusive and values appropriate; pie only for few nonnegative categories.
J09 Time series comparison only when comparable periods; do not invent YoY for missing previous year.
J10 Geographical visuals only when site/country fields valid and mapping confidence high; don't infer country from city alone.
J11 Avoid misleading dual axes, truncated baselines for bar charts, 3D effects, redundant donuts and overloaded dashboards.
J12 Charts and KPI cards must share filter context, denominator definitions and provenance.
J13 No charts built from synthetic filler columns; no 7-chart fixed quota.
J14 Ranking utility score: validity gate first, then business relevance, data quality, information gain, interpretability, visual diversity.
J15 Empty/invalid series => omit chart with explanation, not blank axes or placeholder numbers.
J16 Export static charts only if clearly labeled static; interactive Excel charts must have tested supported controls.
J17 Dashboard page planning: overview, operational drivers, trends, detail, quality/audit. Only create sections supported by data.
J18 Accessibility: contrast, readable labels, patterns/labels not only color, print-friendly layout.

K. EXCEL WORKBOOK OUTPUT CONTRACT
K01 Sheet names generated by detected domain, e.g. Helpdesk Overview, Ticket Details, SLA Analysis, Team Performance, Data Quality, Calculation Audit.
K02 No 'Executive Sales Dashboard' sheet for helpdesk.
K03 Source Data sheet contains original headers and values, with a separate normalized/calculated table; no destructive overwrite.
K04 Excel Tables/dynamic ranges where practical; formulas must work for arbitrary row counts and preserve filtering.
K05 Formula examples (after validated mapping):
 ResolutionHours = IF(OR([@[Finished At]]="",[@[Logged On]]=""),"",([@[Finished At]]-[@[Logged On]])*24)
 SLAMet = IF([@ResolutionHours]="","",IF([@ResolutionHours]<=[@[SLA Limit (hrs)]],1,0))
 NOTE: Excel formulas are illustrative; correct timestamp and cell types must be confirmed. Blank vs zero must be preserved.
K06 Formula error scans: #REF!, #DIV/0!, #VALUE!, #NAME?, #NUM!, broken external links and stale caches.
K07 Currency and date formats follow source locale/explicit choice; never auto-insert USD.
K08 Protect formulas optionally, but allow user inspection and avoid incompatible protection XML; test in Excel desktop/web.
K09 Chart references and caches verified; no stale values from previous generation.
K10 Workbook provenance includes source SHA256, mapping decisions, grain, metric definitions, chart metadata, checks and versions.
K11 Source count, totals and groupings cross-checked after export by independent reader.
K12 Excel workbook file naming reflects dataset/domain and period, never default '_Executive_Sales_Dashboard_2026' for unrelated data.

L. AUDIT CERTIFICATION REPLACEMENT — STRICT EVIDENCE GATES
L01 Replace marketing-style labels with explicit statuses: VERIFIED, PARTIALLY_VERIFIED, NOT_VERIFIED, FAILED, NOT_APPLICABLE.
L02 VERIFIED only when all required applicable source-integrity, mapping, calculation and export checks actually ran and passed.
L03 '100% RECONCILED' forbidden unless 100% of eligible declared metrics reconciled; show counts e.g. 18/20 checks passed.
L04 'CONFIRMED GRAIN' forbidden until key and grain tests pass with evidence.
L05 'CURRENCY PURE' forbidden if no currency measure exists; status NOT_APPLICABLE, not PASS.
L06 'FULL RECALC SAFE' forbidden without Excel-compatible recalculation and cached-value tests.
L07 'SEALED & AUDITED' forbidden as generic label; provide validation timestamp, version and signed/hashed manifest only if truly implemented.
L08 Each audit assertion links to check ID, source hash, expected, actual, tolerance, run timestamp and evidence artifact.
L09 Validation on wrong semantics is failure even if arithmetic reconciles to synthetic numbers.
L10 Reconcile against the RAW upload, never against values generated by the same engine being checked.
L11 Tests not run must show NOT_RUN and cannot count toward verified score.
L12 Tampering/changes after verification invalidate certification unless rerun.
L13 Separate audit of file integrity, semantic interpretation, business calculation, chart selection, export compatibility and privacy.
L14 Display human-readable reasons for exclusions, inferred mappings and user-confirmed assumptions.
L15 Audit sheet must not include sensitive raw samples, secrets or personal identifiers.

M. AI ASSISTANCE BOUNDARY
M01 Optional LLM may propose semantic labels, domain candidates, chart ideas and plain-English explanations; it cannot assert numeric truths.
M02 Never send raw uploads to external AI by default; obtain explicit consent and show data transfer/privacy implications.
M03 Strict schema-constrained AI output, source-column IDs only, no free-form executable formulas or arbitrary code.
M04 Deterministic validator checks AI suggestions against types, units, distributions, constraints and known contradictions.
M05 Low confidence -> user confirmation; unsupported -> omit; conflicting evidence -> neutral profile.
M06 Prompt injection in spreadsheet cells must be treated as data, never instructions.
M07 AI failure, network outage or quota exhaustion must fall back to deterministic generic dashboard.
M08 Store model/version/consent/decision metadata without retaining sensitive payloads unnecessarily.
M09 Explainable decision cards: 'Recognized Finished At as completion timestamp because ...' with confidence and override.
M10 Never call report 'AI-verified' simply because a model generated it.

N. USER EXPERIENCE & PRODUCT DIFFERENTIATION
N01 Upload -> preview original data -> auto-understanding summary -> resolve only blocking ambiguities -> generate -> download.
N02 Show inferred dataset type, row grain, keys, date coverage, missingness, and selected KPIs before export.
N03 'Why this KPI?' displays exact formula, included rows, denominator, filters and source columns.
N04 'Why this chart?' displays rationale, source fields and aggregation.
N05 'Data Quality' screen with actionable fixes and impact estimates; allow exclude/fix with reversible operations.
N06 'No Guessing Mode' enabled by default; any optional speculative insights must be explicitly labeled and off by default.
N07 'Trust Report' shows pass/fail/not-run with real evidence, not badges.
N08 Let users choose between Auto Dashboard and compatible templates; incompatible template produces warning and alternative.
N09 Support guided correction for unusual column headers; ask at most the necessary questions.
N10 Save reusable mapping profiles keyed by structural fingerprint and revalidate against every new file.
N11 One-click regeneration after mapping edits, deterministic output under same inputs/config.
N12 Mobile-friendly upload, schema preview, ambiguity confirmation, export progress and download.
N13 Shareable sample demos with synthetic data; never accidentally expose uploads publicly.
N14 Explain unsupported metrics instead of quietly hiding user-requested results.

O. SECURITY / PRIVACY / RESOURCE SAFETY
O01 CSV/Excel formula injection sanitization for untrusted text beginning =,+,-,@ where interpreted as formulas, preserving raw value separately.
O02 ZIP bombs, XML entity expansion, huge shared strings, malformed formulas, embedded hyperlinks, external references and macros.
O03 File size 50 MB upload limit plus decompressed bytes, cells, rows, columns and runtime limits.
O04 Memory-safe parsing, background workers, cancellation, progress reporting, timeout and retry.
O05 Sensitive field detection (email, phone, ID, addresses, health-like notes), data minimization, export warnings and masking options.
O06 No cross-tenant data mixing; avoid reusing cached mappings or totals across users/uploads.
O07 Source content cannot instruct agent behavior; never execute formulas/macros/scripts from uploaded file.
O08 Privacy promises must match actual architecture, telemetry, processing location and retention policy.
O09 Download expiry and access controls when files touch servers; delete temporary files per documented retention.
O10 Error logging redacts raw data and sensitive column examples.

P. PERFORMANCE AND SCALABILITY
P01 Baseline 8k rows, 12 cols, <1 MB CSV; record parsing, profiling, inference, aggregation, charts, export timings separately.
P02 20-second generation is an aspiration until tested across devices and formats; don't promise fixed latency universally.
P03 Stress tests 100, 8k, 100k, 500k rows and wide tables within file/memory limits.
P04 Streaming column profiling, sketches/approximate distinct only when labeled, exact final metrics when feasible.
P05 Cache by source hash + engine version + mapping version + user permissions; invalidate on schema/mapping changes.
P06 Limit chart cardinality and worksheet formatting overhead; avoid per-cell expensive operations.
P07 Provide partial preview while full report computes; never show provisional numbers as verified.
P08 Document browser/device-specific resource limits.

